r high-volume distributed systems that need chronological order and a 27-character URL-safe string."},"574":{"id":"/docs/reference/nanoid","page_id":"/docs/reference/nanoid","type":"page","content":"Nanoid","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/nanoid"},"575":{"id":"/docs/reference/nanoid-0","page_id":"/docs/reference/nanoid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/nanoid","content":"Compact, URL-safe random strings with configurable length and alphabet."},"576":{"id":"/docs/reference/nanoid-1","page_id":"/docs/reference/nanoid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/nanoid","content":"Nanoid is the compact choice for short URLs, invite codes, and session tokens. Its default alphabet is URL-safe; pass a length or options only when the boundary needs a different shape."},"577":{"id":"/docs/reference/nanoid-2","page_id":"/docs/reference/nanoid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/nanoid","content":"`nanoid.isValid()` validates the default alphabet, not values generated with a custom alphabet."},"578":{"id":"/docs/reference/objectid","page_id":"/docs/reference/objectid","type":"page","content":"ObjectID","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/objectid"},"579":{"id":"/docs/reference/objectid-0","page_id":"/docs/reference/objectid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/objectid","content":"MongoDB-compatible 12-byte identifiers."},"580":{"id":"/docs/reference/objectid-1","page_id":"/docs/reference/objectid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/objectid","content":"ObjectID matches MongoDB's native 12-byte representation. Use it when your application stores `_id` values alongside MongoDB clients or needs to preserve that wire format."},"581":{"id":"/docs/reference/tsid","page_id":"/docs/reference/tsid","type":"page","content":"TSID","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/tsid"},"582":{"id":"/docs/reference/tsid-0","page_id":"/docs/reference/tsid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/tsid","content":"Time-sorted 64-bit identifiers for native bigint storage."},"583":{"id":"/docs/reference/tsid-1","page_id":"/docs/reference/tsid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/tsid","content":"TSID returns a `bigint`, not a string. Use it when a database `BIGINT` primary key is the natural representation and you still need time ordering without a central allocator."},"584":{"id":"/docs/reference/typeid","page_id":"/docs/reference/typeid","type":"page","content":"TypeID","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/typeid"},"585":{"id":"/docs/reference/typeid-0","page_id":"/docs/reference/typeid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/typeid","content":"UUID v7 identifiers with a readable, domain-specific prefix."},"586":{"id":"/docs/reference/typeid-1","page_id":"/docs/reference/typeid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/typeid","content":"TypeID wraps a UUID v7 suffix in a lowercase prefix such as `user_` or `order_`. It is useful when a public identifier should reveal the kind of resource it identifies."},"587":{"id":"/docs/reference/ulid","page_id":"/docs/reference/ulid","type":"page","content":"ULID","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/ulid"},"588":{"id":"/docs/reference/ulid-0","page_id":"/docs/reference/ulid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/ulid","content":"Sortable, URL-safe identifiers using Crockford Base32."},"589":{"id":"/docs/reference/ulid-1","page_id":"/docs/reference/ulid","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/reference/ulid","content":"ULIDs sort by creation time and stay shorter than a hyphenated UUID. Use them when the value will often appear in URLs, logs, or APIs."},"590":{"id":"/docs/reference/uuid-v4","page_id":"/docs/reference/uuid-v4","type":"page","content":"UUID v4","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Reference"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/reference/uuid-v4"},"591":{"id":"/docs/reference/uuid-v4-0","